          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em homotopy groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(X,x)
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+object"} pointed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,x)
            are a sequence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
            that generalise the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(X,x)
            to higher
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+%28as+a+transformation%29"} homotopies
          %p
            The
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(X,x)
            has as elements
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+classes"} equivalence classes
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spheres"} spheres
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                  %mo :
                  %msubsup
                    %mi S
                    %mo *
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma : S_*^n \to X_*
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            where two such are regarded as equivalent if there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+homotopy"} left homotopy
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi γ
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma \Rightarrow \gamma'
            between them, fixing the base point. The group operation is given by gluing of two spheres at their basepoint.
          %p
            In degree 0,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0(X,x)
            is not a group but merely a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+set"} pointed set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 2
            all homotopy groups are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(X,x)
            may be an arbitrary group. In general,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(X,x)
            is an
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/k-tuply+groupal+n-groupoid"} tuply groupal
            set.
          %p
            Lower homotopy groups
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} act
            on higher homotopy groups; the nonabelian group cohomology of this gives the Postnikov invariants of the space. All of this data put together allows one to reconstruct the original space, at least up to weak
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Postnikov+system"} Postnikov system
          %p
            These definitions only depend on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalence"} weak homotopy equivalence
            between topological spaces is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+map"} continuous map
            that induces an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            on all homotopy groups.
          %p
            Accordingly, homotopy groups are defined for all other models of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+homotopy+group"} simplicial homotopy group
            for more.
          %p
            There are generalizations of the concept to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+groups"} stable homotopy groups
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectra"} spectra
            and to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+homotopy+groups"} equivariant homotopy groups
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+G-spaces"} topological G-spaces
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+spectra"} equivariant spectra
          %h2#Definition Definition
          %h3#ForTopologicalSpaces For topological spaces
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            be a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x : * \to X
            be a point, to be called the
            = succeed "." do
              %em base point
          %p
            For
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^n
            be the pointed
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sphere"} sphere
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              The underlying set of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(X,x)
              is the set of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+classes"} equivalence classes
              of basepoint-preserving
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+functions"} continuous functions
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi γ
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\gamma : S^n \to X
            %p
              where two such are regarded as equivalent if there is a basepoint-preserving
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+homotopy"} left homotopy
              between them.
          %p Now we will put some structure on that set.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              There are
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              independent equators through the basepoint of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^n
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo :
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mi n
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f, g: S^n \to (X,x)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproduct"} copairing
              in the category of pointed spaces to get a map
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo ∨
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    S^n \vee S^n \to (X,x)
            %p
              (where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∨
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vee
              indicates the
              = succeed ");" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wedge+sum"} wedge sum
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo ∨
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^n \to S^n \vee S^n
              that maps the
              = succeed "th" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mi n
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^n \to (X,x)
              = succeed "th" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              %strong concatenation
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              and
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %msub
                      %mo →
                      %mi i
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo ∨
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi f
                        %mo ,
                        %mi g
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    S^n \to_i S^n \vee S^n \stackrel{[f,g]}{\to} (X,x)
                    \\,.
            %p
              One can check that each of these operations respects homotopy equivalence and hence equips
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(X,x)
              with the structure of a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magma"} magma
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              These magmas are in fact
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
            %ul
              %li
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constant+function"} constant function
                that maps all of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^n
                to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                represents the
                %strong null element
                of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo ,
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(X,x)
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              This is closely related to the statement that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+dimension+spheres+are+H-cogroup+objects"} positive dimension spheres are H-cogroup objects
              (see there) in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+topological+spaces"} pointed topological spaces
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              This may seem like quite a complicated kind of structure, but it is actually quite simple up to homotopy. First of all, all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              concatenations of given maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
              are homotopic, so we speak of simply a single
              %strong concatenation
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 1
              (and none for
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 0
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eckmann-Hilton+argument"} Eckmann-Hilton argument
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 2
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associativity"} associative
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse"} invertible
              up to homotopy, and the null element is an identity up to homotopy.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              The result is that the set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(X,x)
              of equivalence classes is an abelian group for
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 2
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 0
              (when the null element is the only structure).
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+space"} path-connected
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(X,a)
              are
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphic
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(X)
              in that case. (This is why we must use
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_n(X)
              for the homotopy
              = succeed "-groupoid.)" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(X,a)
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(X,b)
              (given by
              = succeed ")," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_{n+1}(X)
          %h3#for_simplicial_sets For simplicial sets
          %p
            See
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+homotopy+group"} simplicial homotopy group
          %h3#for_lie_groupoids For Lie groupoids
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups+of+a+Lie+groupoid"} homotopy groups of a Lie groupoid
          %h3#for_objects_in_a_general_stack_topos
            For objects in a general
            = succeed "-stack" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "-topos" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            is the archetypical
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
          %p
            The definition of homotopy groups for objects in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            is just a special case of a general definition of homotopy groups of objects of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-stack"} ∞-stack
            = succeed "es." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
          %p This is described in detail at
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"}> homotopy groups in an (∞,1)-topos
              \.
          %h2#truncconn Truncated and connected spaces
          %p
            Often it is useful to talk about spaces whose homotopy groups are all trivial above or below a certain degree, for instance in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Postnikov+towers"} Postnikov towers
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead+towers"} Whitehead towers
          %p
            For
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = -1, 0, 1, 2, \ldots, \infty
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                a space is
                %strong
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                  \-
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+n-type"} truncated
                if all homotopy groups
                %em above
                degree
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                are trivial.
            %li
              %p
                a space is
                %strong
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                  \-
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-connected+space"} connected
                (or
                = succeed ")" do
                  %strong
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                    \-simply connected
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+space"} inhabited
                and all homotopy groups
                %em at or below
                degree
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                are trivial.
          %p
            Vacuously, every space is
            = succeed "-truncated," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "-connected." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contractible+spaces"} contractible spaces
            are
            = succeed "-connected," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "-truncated" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-1)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classically
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            = succeed "-truncated" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+space"} empty
            or weakly contractible.)
          %p
            To extend one step further in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/negative+thinking"} negative thinking
            = succeed "-connected," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-2)
            = succeed "-truncated." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-2)
          %ul
            %li
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+space"} pointed space
              is a
              %strong
                degree-
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-Mac+Lane+space"} Eilenberg-Mac Lane space
              if it is both
              = succeed "-truncated" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              = succeed "-connected," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            A weakly contractible space is an Eilenberg–MacLane space in every degree, and these are the only Eilenberg–MacLane spaces in degree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -1
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+space"} discrete spaces
            (and those weakly homotopy equivalent to such). In degree
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+spaces"} classifying spaces
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#in_low_dimensions In low dimensions
          %p
            The
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0(X,a)
            can be identified with the set of all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+component"} path components
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            as the basepoint. Similarly, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+infinity-groupoid"} fundamental 0-groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_0(X)
            is the set of all path components without a chosen basepoint. Note that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_0(X)
            is traditionally written
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0(X)
          %p
            The
            = succeed "st" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(X,a)
            is precisely the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            at
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi(X,a)
            with the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            standing for Poincaré, who invented it.
          .query
            %p
              At least, that's where I
              %em think
              that it comes from … —Toby
          %h3#of_the_circle Of the circle
          %p
            The first homotopy group of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            is the group of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integer"} integers
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\pi_1(S^1) \simeq \mathbb{Z}
                  \\,.
          %p
            A formalization of a proof of this in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            is in (
            %a{:href => "#ShulmanPi1S1"}> Shulman
            ).
          %h3#of_spheres Of spheres
          %p
            See
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups+of+spheres"} homotopy groups of spheres
          %h2#history History
          %p
            In the early years of the 20th century it was known that the nonabelian fundamental group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(X,a)
            of a space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            with base point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            was useful in geometry and complex analysis. It was also known that the abelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology+group"} homology groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_n(X)
            existed for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 0
            and that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is connected then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_1(X)
            is isomorphic to the abelianisation of any
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(X,a)
          %p Consequently it was hoped to generalise the fundamental group to higher dimensions, producing nonabelian groups whose abelianisations would be the homology groups.
          %p
            In 1932, E. Čech proposed a definition of higher homotopy groups using maps of spheres, but the paper was rejected for the Zurich ICM since it was found that these groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(X,a)
            were abelian for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 2
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalence"} weak homotopy equivalence
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
            It was early realised that the fundamental groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1(X)
            operates on the family of groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\pi_n(X,a) | a \in X\}
            which should thus together be regarded as a module over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(X,a)
          %p
            A key property of homotopy groups is the
            = succeed ":" do
              %em Whitehead theorem
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:X \to Y
            is a map of connected
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/m-cofibrant+spaces"} m-cofibrant spaces
            (spaces each of the homotopy type of a
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CW+complex"} CW complex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            induces isomorphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(X,a) \to \pi_n(Y,f(a))
            for some
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} homotopy equivalence
          %p
            However, the homotopy groups by themselves, even considering the operations of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+homotopy"} algebraic homotopy theory
          %p
            See also the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freudenthal+suspension+theorem"} Freudenthal suspension theorem
          %h2#some_general_nonsense Some general nonsense
          %p
            Using the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eckmann-Hilton+duality"} Eckmann-Hilton duality
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+%28as+an+operation%29"} homotopy (as an operation)
            one may discuss homotopy groups along the same lines as the discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+groups"} cohomology groups
            (see there).
          %p From that perspective we might say that:
          %p
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B, X
            any two objects in an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            with co-coefficients
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-set"} hom-set
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\pi_H(X,B)
                  \\coloneqq
                  H(B,X)
                  \\coloneqq
                  \\pi_0 \mathbf{H}(B,X)
                  \\,,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_H
            denotes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category+of+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} homotopy category
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
          %p
            For the special case that the object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            here is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/co-group+object"} co-group object
            %em homotopy set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi(X,B)
            naturally inherits the structure of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
          %p
            The standard example is that where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B = S^n
            is the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sphere"} sphere
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+object"} interval object
            and more generally that underlying the mechanism of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Trimble+n-category"} Trimble n-category
          %p
            As opposed to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            where people are used to talking about
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+cohomology"} generalized cohomology
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B = S^n
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+group+of+a+spectrum"} homotopy group of a spectrum
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalence"} weak homotopy equivalence
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-localization"} p-localization
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Goodwillie+calculus"} Goodwillie calculus
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} homotopy groups in an (infinity,1)-topos
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+homotopy+type"}> finite homotopy type
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nilpotent+homotopy+type"} nilpotent homotopy type
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-local+homotopy+type"} p-local homotopy type
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-complete+homotopy+type"} p-complete homotopy type
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+homotopy+group"} equivariant homotopy group
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p The oiginal definition:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Witold+Hurewicz"}> Witold Hurewicz
              \:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Beiträge zur Topologie der Deformationen (I. Höherdimensionale Homotopiegruppen)
              %strong 38
              (1935) 112–119 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://dwc.knaw.nl/DL/publications/PU00016667.pdf"}> pdf
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Hurewicz-BeitraegeI.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p
            On the early history of the notion of the higher homotopy groups (beyond the
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Hilton"}> Peter Hilton
              , p. 289 (9 of 11) in:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Subjective History of Homology and Homotopy Theory
              %strong 61
              5 (1988) 282-291 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2307/2689545"}> doi:10.2307/2689545
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %blockquote
            %p
              “At a meeting in Vienna in 1931
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eduard+%C4%8Cech"} Čech
              gave a paper in which he described certain groups from the homotopy point of view. He had no applications of these groups. Moreover, he had only one theorem, that they were
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} commutative
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pavel+Aleksandrov"} Alexandroff
              played a role here, that they could not be interesting, because it was thought that any information that could be obtained from
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} Abelian groups
              must come from the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+homology"} homology
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Witold+Hurewicz"} Hurewicz
              redefined the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
              and immediately gave important applications in a series of four notes which were intended as preliminary publications. In that series of four papers he showed the significance of what we now call
              = succeed ".\u201D" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/obstruction+theory"} obstruction theory
          %p
            Modern accounts (for more see at
            = succeed "):" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Goerss"}> Paul Goerss
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J.+F.+Jardine"} J. F. Jardine
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simplicial+homotopy+theory"} Simplicial homotopy theory
            %li#AGP02
              %p
                Marcelo Aguilar,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Samuel+Gitler"} Samuel Gitler
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Algebraic topology from a homotopical viewpoint
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://tocs.ulb.tu-darmstadt.de/106999419.pdf"} toc pdf
            %li#Hutchings
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Hutchings"}> Michael Hutchings
                ,
                %em Introduction to higher homotopy groups and obstruction theory
                (
                %a{:href => "http://math.berkeley.edu/~hutching/teach/215b-2011/homotopy.pdf"}> pdf
                )
          %p
            With an eye towards application in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+physics"} mathematical physics
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mikio+Nakahara"}> Mikio Nakahara
              , Chapter 4 of:
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Geometry%2C+Topology+and+Physics"} Geometry, Topology and Physics
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1201/9781315275826"} doi:10.1201/9781315275826
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://alpha.sinp.msu.ru/~panov/LibBooks/GRAV/(Graduate_Student_Series_in_Physics)Mikio_Nakahara-Geometry,_Topology_and_Physics,_Second_Edition_(Graduate_Student_Series_in_Physics)-Institute_of_Physics_Publishing(2003).pdf"} pdf
          %h3#formalization_in_hott Formalization in HoTT
          %p
            Homotopy groups and their properties can naturally be formalized in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(S^1)\simeq \mathbb{Z}
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The HoTT Coq-HoTT library:
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://github.com/HoTT/Coq-HoTT/blob/master/theories/Homotopy/Pi1S1.v"} Pi1S1.v
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Licata"}> Dan Licata
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"} Mike Shulman
                %em Calculating the fundamental group of the circle in homotopy type theory
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.cs.cmu.edu/~drl/pubs/ls13pi1s1/ls13pi1s1.pdf"}> pdf
                )
          %p and a proof that
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mrow
                    %mo {
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mi for
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi k
                            %mo &lt;
                            %mi n
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi ℤ
                          %mtd
                            %mi for
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi k
                            %mo =
                            %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_k(S^n) \simeq \left\{ \array{ 0 &amp; for \;k \lt n \\ \mathbb{Z} &amp; for\; k = n} \right.
          %p is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Licata"}> Dan Licata
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Guillaume+Brunerie"} Guillaume Brunerie
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://github.com/dlicata335/hott-agda/blob/master/homotopy/PiNSN.agda"} PiNSN.agda
        .revisedby
